+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for concurrent edits to the same visit
+
+## Context
+
+A visit is a single plot observation recorded by a field worker. The same visit is often
+corrected twice, independently, by workers who were in the field at different times and
+compared notes afterwards. Tablets are offline for up to 48 hours, so both corrections can
+arrive at the server days after the state they were based on was last seen by anyone. The
+team of two coordinators resolves the resulting conflict, and a regional science partner
+receives a CSV of reviewed visits every Friday.
+
+Two properties make this decision load-bearing rather than a detail. First, no submitted edit
+may be discarded: the existing spreadsheet import silently overwrites corrections, which is
+the problem the system exists to remove. Second, the server must not treat arrival order as
+evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative — the two coordinators are
+the only ones positioned to judge which correction is scientifically right, and the store is
+not.
+
+The store therefore needs a rule for what happens when two edits to the same visit arrive
+that were both based on the same earlier state. This decision settles that rule, and it
+settles it early: tablet-sync and review-export are sized around it, and both designs wait
+on it.
+
+Standing constraints every option respects, and which therefore do not discriminate between
+them:
+
+- No accepted submission may be discarded, whichever outcome the reconciliation produces.
+- Arrival order alone never determines which edit becomes the visit's current state.
+- Two coordinators, without a dedicated systems administrator, must be able to operate the
+  resulting workflow end to end.
+- The exported CSV must be explainable back to the submissions it came from.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate — no automatic inference of scientific relationships between fields.**
+Automatic reconciliation must not combine two authors' edits on the grounds that they concern
+the same scientific question — for example, treating a revised species identification and a
+revised cover estimate for that species as one edit rather than two. This gate is inherited
+from the project's non-goal on automated botanical validation and inference of scientific
+intent: no party in this project is positioned to author and maintain rules that encode
+those relationships, and no field worker can be asked to trust an unseen rule that has
+silently joined their correction to a stranger's.
+
+**Criterion (major) — risk of a wrong combination reaching the partner.** How likely is it
+that an exported value differs from what either worker intended, and how likely is it that
+nobody notices before the Friday export?
+
+**Criterion (major) — review workload the two coordinators must absorb.** How much of their
+weekly capacity goes to comparing competing edits, and how much of it goes to ordinary
+review?
+
+**Criterion (moderate) — correctness logic the team must maintain.** How much domain logic
+must be kept correct over time, by people whose day job is fieldwork, as the set of fields
+and species entries changes?
+
+**Criterion (moderate) — offline submission size and tablet storage.** What a tablet must
+carry and re-send for each edit, under a 48-hour disconnection.
+
+**Criterion (moderate) — export completeness at the Friday deadline.** How much of a
+season's visits reach the partner on time, and what is exported in their place when a visit
+is not settled.
+
+**Criterion (minor) — provenance of an exported value.** How readily a reader can trace an
+exported field back to the worker and submission it came from.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every edit is stored as a complete revision of the visit, and every edit
+names the revision it was based on. The store keeps the whole submitted visit as one
+immutable revision with its author and receipt time. When an edit arrives whose base is
+still the visit's current revision, that edit becomes the new current revision and nothing
+needs review. When an edit arrives whose base has since moved on — because another
+revision superseded it, or because the edit names no base at all — the edit is stored as a
+competing revision and the visit is marked as having unresolved review work. A coordinator
+compares the competing revisions against the current one and selects which revision becomes
+the visit's state. Every revision is retained: the ones not selected are marked superseded
+but remain readable, with their authors, so nothing a worker submitted disappears from the
+record. No field of a visit is ever interpreted against another field's rules, because no
+field-level rules exist.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and Worker B both open visit `9f1c…` from revision `r0`, which
+shows *Aster tripolium* at 40% cover with observer *D. Okafor*. A submits a revision `r1`
+naming base `r0`; it becomes current. B submits a revision `r2` also naming base `r0`,
+correcting the species to *Aster tripolium* subsp. *tripolium* and the cover to 55%. `r2` is
+stored as a competing revision and the visit is flagged unresolved. The coordinator is shown
+`r1` and `r2` side by side and selects one; the other is retained as superseded history. If
+A then edits the visit again, their tablet carries base `r1` and the new edit is ordinary —
+unless a coordinator has since resolved the visit, in which case the tablet's base is stale
+again and the edit becomes competing work.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No automatic inference of scientific relationships between fields: **passes.** The store
+  compares revision identifiers, never field contents.
+- Risk of a wrong combination reaching the partner: **strong fit.** No value is ever
+  assembled from two workers' edits; each exported visit is one worker's revision, and the
+  choice between revisions is made by a person.
+- Review workload the two coordinators must absorb: **weak fit.** Every edit made against a
+  stale base becomes review work, including edits to fields that have nothing to do with one
+  another — a corrected observer name alongside an added species still produces a queue item.
+- Correctness logic the team must maintain: **strong fit.** There are no per-field rules to
+  maintain. The logic is revision lineage and resolution, which do not change as fields are
+  added.
+- Offline submission size and tablet storage: **weak fit.** Each edit re-sends the complete
+  visit payload, and each tablet must retain the base revision identifier, so payload size
+  and stored state both grow with the size of a visit.
+- Export completeness at the Friday deadline: **partial fit.** Visits with competing
+  revisions are held out of the export until a coordinator settles them, so the partner
+  receives fewer visits on time; the visit is not exported in a guessed state instead.
+- Provenance of an exported value: **strong fit.** Every exported visit is a single stored
+  revision with one author.
+
+**Summary.** Keeps each worker's visit intact as one unit, at the cost of a review queue
+that grows with the number of concurrent edits and of export rows held back until resolved.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Every edit supplies the base state it started from together with a
+structured description of the fields it changed. The store combines the base, the first
+edit, and the second edit field by field: where the two edits touch different fields, the
+combined result is assembled automatically and becomes the current revision with no review;
+where they touch the same field, or where a relationship between fields makes the
+combination questionable — a species identification and its cover estimate, an observer list
+and a note naming that observer — the store defers to review. The rules that decide which
+fields are independent, and how to combine list-valued fields such as observers and species
+entries, are maintained per field type over time. The combined result carries provenance
+per field, so a reviewer can see which worker contributed which value. The store holds a
+base snapshot per visit to merge against.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and Worker B both start from revision `r0` of visit `9f1c…`. A
+changes the observer list to add *R. Silva*; B changes the cover of *Aster tripolium* from
+40% to 55%. The store combines them into `r3` — observers `D. Okafor, R. Silva`, cover 55%
+— and marks the visit resolved, with the cover attributed to B and the observer to A. No
+coordinator sees it. If instead A revises the species identification and B revises that
+species' cover estimate, the rules declare the pair related, the combination is withheld,
+and the coordinator decides.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No automatic inference of scientific relationships between fields: **eliminated.** The
+  option's core is a rules engine that decides which fields are independent; a rules engine
+  that treats a species identification and its cover estimate as independent is inferring a
+  scientific relationship, and the rules that get it right are the ones a conservation team
+  is not positioned to write and maintain.
+- Risk of a wrong combination reaching the partner: **weak fit.** A rules engine that
+  wrongly treats two related edits as independent produces a combined visit that no worker
+  ever described and that no coordinator sees, and the error is silent until someone notices
+  the science in the CSV.
+- Review workload the two coordinators must absorb: **strong fit.** Only genuinely
+  overlapping edits reach review.
+- Correctness logic the team must maintain: **weak fit.** Rules are needed for scalar
+  fields, for observer lists, and for species entries, and each new field or species
+  structure is another rule to write, test, and keep correct.
+- Offline submission size and tablet storage: **strong fit.** The tablet sends a structured
+  change set rather than a whole visit, and stores a base snapshot per visit it edits.
+- Export completeness at the Friday deadline: **strong fit.** Combined visits resolve
+  without a coordinator, so more visits are export-ready on Friday.
+- Provenance of an exported value: **partial fit.** Provenance exists, but it is per field
+  and per contributing edit, so explaining a value requires walking the merge history rather
+  than naming a submission.
+
+**Summary.** Keeps the coordinator queue small and the export large, at the cost of a
+maintained rules engine that decides which edits belong together.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions are selected.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet *Review over silent combination*: where a mechanism could
+reconcile two submissions automatically, prefer a visible review queue over a rule that
+combines them, unless that rule is provably safe for the fields it touches. Field-aware
+three-way merge fails that test for exactly the fields the project exists to protect.
+Scientific relatedness between a species identification and its cover estimate is not
+provable from field names, and the rules that would encode it would be maintained by people
+not positioned to write them — the gate forbids it, and the tenet asks for the review queue
+that replaces it.
+
+The option wins outright on two major-to-moderate criteria as well: it eliminates the
+maintained correctness logic the team would otherwise carry, and it removes the risk of an
+unnoticed wrong combination reaching the partner. It loses on review workload, submission
+size, and export completeness, and those losses are the price the project accepts. The
+rejected option is eliminated by a gate rather than out-scored, and gates are not a matter
+of degree.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Automatic reconciliation of concurrent edits. Automatic combining
+of disjoint field changes is not available to the project, and no downstream node may
+introduce it. A tablet that holds a stale base revision cannot have its edit applied
+automatically, however far the edit is from the work already on the server.
+
+**What this obliges downstream.** Two sibling nodes are sized by this decision, and their
+designs wait on the contract this node publishes:
+
+- *tablet-sync* must keep, per visit it edits, the identifier of the revision the edit was
+  based on, and must re-send that identifier with every submission. It must retain the
+  complete visit payload locally to build each submission, and must handle three server
+  outcomes distinctly: the edit became the current revision; the edit was stored as
+  competing work and the visit awaits a coordinator; the submission was rejected and the
+  tablet must fetch current state and resubmit. It must show a worker that their edit is
+  awaiting review rather than applied, and it must never present a queued edit as settled.
+- *review-export* must present competing revisions side by side for a visit, with each
+  revision's author and the fields where the revisions differ, and must let a coordinator
+  select the revision that becomes the visit's state. It must hold unresolved visits out of
+  the CSV entirely rather than export a guess, and must be able to name, for each exported
+  visit, the revision and author it came from.
+
+**When to reopen this decision.** The gate that eliminates field-aware three-way merge would
+fall away if rules with a scientific owner became available — for example, a rule set
+maintained by the regional science partner, written by a botanist, whose independence
+judgements could be reviewed before use. The trade-off also moves if the review queue
+exceeds what two coordinators can clear before the weekly export, or if holding unresolved
+visits out of the CSV starts costing the partner more than an occasional bad row would. Both
+are visible in operation, and either is grounds to reopen this document rather than to
+work around it inside the nodes.
