+Nothing accepted is ever deleted.
+
+A revision is an immutable, complete copy of a visit's fields. The visit's fields are the
+plot reference, observation date, observer names, species cover estimates, and notes. A
+revision names the revision it was based on, its *base revision*; a revision with no base
+is a first submission or an edit made without any starting point.
+
+The store keeps two things per visit: the **current revision**, which is the visit's state
+as the server understands it, and the set of **competing revisions**, which are edits that
+arrived against a base other than the current revision. A visit with at least one competing
+revision has **unresolved review work**.
+
+| Operation | Purpose | Principal inputs | Result |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Submit revision | Accept a worker's edit of a visit | visit ID, base revision ID (optional), visit fields, idempotency key | Revision ID, receipt time, whether the visit now has unresolved review work |
+| Get visit | Read the visit's current state | visit ID | Current revision with its fields, author, and receipt time; unresolved flag; competing revision count |
+| Get revision | Read one stored revision in full | revision ID | Revision fields, author, receipt time, base revision ID, state |
+| List visit history | Read every revision of a visit in receipt order | visit ID | Revisions with lineage and state |
+| List review work | Read the coordinator's queue | — | Visits with unresolved work: current revision and every competing revision, each with author and receipt time |
+| Resolve review work | Record a coordinator's decision | visit ID, selected revision ID | Resolution record: coordinator, time, selected revision, contributing and superseded revision IDs |
+| List visits | Enumerate visits for export | Filter: unresolved or resolved | Visit IDs with their current revision and unresolved flag |
+
+## Visit Revisions
+
+Revisions of one visit form a tree. Each revision points at its base; a revision with no
+base is the root. The current revision is the tip the store has accepted; competing
+revisions are branches hanging off it or off one of its ancestors.
+
+A revision moves between three states:
+
+```mermaid
+stateDiagram-v2
+    [*] --> current: accepted against the current revision
+    [*] --> competing: accepted against any other base
+    competing --> current: selected by a coordinator
+    competing --> superseded: not selected by a coordinator
+    current --> superseded: replaced by a later submission or by a resolution
+    superseded --> [*]: retained for history
+```
+
+Superseded revisions stay readable with their authors and their fields. A superseded
+revision is one that is no longer the visit's state, not one that has been thrown away.
+
+## Submission
+
+A submission carries the visit ID, the base revision ID the worker edited from, the visit
+fields, and an idempotency key. The store authenticates the worker, validates the field
+types and the plot reference against the catalog, and then applies the acceptance rules
+below. The server assigns the revision ID, the author, and the receipt time; a client
+cannot supply them.
+
+A submission is a complete copy of the visit, not a description of changed fields. A tablet
+that edits a visit re-sends every field of that visit together with the base revision ID.
+
+The idempotency key belongs to the submitting worker. A retry that presents an accepted
+key with an identical submission — same visit, same base revision, same fields — returns
+the original acceptance result and creates no second revision. Presenting an accepted key
+with a different submission is rejected and leaves the original revision unchanged.
+
+### Acceptance Rules
+
+1. A submission whose base revision is the visit's current revision becomes the new current
+   revision. The visit has no competing revisions afterwards, and the previous current
+   revision is retained as superseded.
+2. A submission whose base revision is any other revision of the same visit becomes a
+   competing revision, and the visit gains unresolved review work.
+3. A submission with no base revision for a visit that does not yet exist creates the visit:
+   the revision becomes its current revision.
+4. A submission with no base revision for a visit that already exists becomes a competing
+   revision, and the visit gains unresolved review work. An edit made without any starting
+   point does not claim to be based on the visit's state.
+5. A submission whose base revision is not a revision of that visit is rejected, and no
+   revision is stored. The rejection names the visit's current revision so the client can
+   fetch current state and resubmit.
+
+Rule 5 keeps a client and the store from drifting apart silently: a base revision the store
+has never seen means the client is working from a state the store does not hold.
+
+## Current State and Retrieval
+
+Retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work. A visit's
+current revision is returned whether or not the visit is unresolved, together with an
+unresolved flag and the number of competing revisions. A visit that has never been
+submitted has no current revision.
+
+While a visit has competing revisions, its current revision remains the state the store has
+accepted and does not advance on account of the competing revisions. Arrival order does not
+promote a revision to current state.
+
+## Review Work and Resolution
+
+Review work for a visit consists of the visit's current revision together with all of its
+competing revisions. These are the candidate revisions. The coordinator compares them and
+selects one candidate; the selected revision becomes the visit's current revision and every
+other candidate becomes superseded. The visit then has no unresolved review work.
+
+A coordinator may select the current revision itself, which settles the visit in favour of
+the state already accepted and rejects the competing edits without discarding them.
+
+A resolution record retains the coordinator's identity, the resolution time, the selected
+revision, the identifiers of the contributing revisions and their submissions, and the
+identifiers of the revisions it superseded. Resolutions do not create revisions of their
+own; a coordinator's edit to a visit is a submission like any other, submitted by the
+coordinator's identity.
+
+## Export Eligibility
+
+A visit is eligible for export when it has a current revision and no competing revisions.
+The store reports eligibility as the unresolved flag so that review-export can hold
+unresolved visits out of the CSV rather than export a guess.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Reconciling concurrent edits to one visit | Whole-visit revisions: each edit is stored as a complete revision naming its base, and edits based on a superseded base become competing revisions for a coordinator to settle | Field-aware three-way merge with maintained field-combination rules | Merge rules would have to decide which edits are scientifically related, which no party here is positioned to maintain; the risk of a silent wrong combination outweighs the smaller review queue. See `decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md`. |
+| A revision's state while it is not current | Exactly three states — current, competing, superseded — with superseded revisions retained and readable | Deleting the losing revision on resolution | A discarded edit is the failure the system exists to prevent; the exported state must stay traceable to every submission. |
+| Whether an edit with no base revision can be stored against an existing visit | Yes: it is stored as a competing revision | Rejecting it, forcing the client to name a base | Rejection risks stranding an offline edit whose base the tablet cannot supply; storing it keeps the worker's visit intact and puts it in front of a coordinator. |
+| A submission naming a base revision the store has never held | Rejected, with no revision stored | Accepting it as a competing revision | A client and store that disagree about history produce a comparison the coordinator cannot read; the rejection tells the client to fetch current state. |
+| What the current revision is while a visit has competing revisions | The revision the store has accepted; competing revisions do not advance it | Treating the most recently received revision as current | Arrival order is not evidence of authority; the visit is held out of export until settled, so no one acts on the provisional state. |
+| Whether a coordinator may author new content while resolving | No: a coordinator selects one candidate revision; any further edit is an ordinary submission under the coordinator's identity | Letting the coordinator write a merged revision at resolution time | A coordinator-authored revision adds a third origin of visit content and complicates the lineage; for the first release, selecting among submitted revisions is enough. |
+| How a retry is recognised | By idempotency key scoped to the submitting worker, compared against the whole submission (visit, base revision, fields) | Scoping the key globally, or comparing only the fields | The key identifies one client's attempt at one edit; a different submission under the same key is a conflict, not a retry. |
+| Fields the store interprets | None: the store compares revision identifiers and never interprets one field against another | Per-field rules for combining or validating edits | Field-level rules encode scientific judgements the project does not make; keeping the store ignorant of field semantics keeps that judgement with the coordinators. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Concurrent edits to the same visit are reconciled as whole-visit revisions with
+   coordinator resolution; field-aware merging was rejected. Rationale and the conditions for
+   reopening it are in `decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md`.
+2. Every accepted revision is retained; nothing is deleted by a resolution.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. How long revisions and resolution records are retained, and whether any archival or
+   compaction applies to a season's visits.
+2. Whether a coordinator may revise a visit at all while resolving competing revisions, as
+   opposed to only selecting among them.
+3. Whether a visit may be reopened — for example, when a coordinator discovers after the
+   Friday export that a settled visit was wrong — and what reopening does to a visit already
+   exported.
+4. Whether the two coordinators may also act as submitting workers, which would let a
+   coordinator's own revision appear in a review queue.
+
+## Downstream Contract
+
+Two sibling nodes are sized by this node's contract and are designed after it.
+
+*tablet-sync* keeps, per visit it edits, the identifier of the revision the edit was based
+on, and sends that identifier with every submission. It retains the complete visit locally
+so that it can re-send whole visits. It distinguishes three server outcomes: the edit became
+the current revision, the edit was stored as competing work and the visit awaits a
+coordinator, and the submission was rejected so the tablet must fetch current state and
+resubmit. A worker is shown that an edit is awaiting review rather than applied. A tablet
+never presents an edit as settled while the server holds competing revisions for the visit.
+
+*review-export* presents the competing revisions of a visit beside the current revision,
+with each revision's author, receipt time, and the fields where the revisions differ, and
+lets a coordinator select the revision that becomes the visit's state. It holds unresolved
+visits out of the CSV entirely and names, for each exported visit, the revision and the
+author it came from.
